<section>
  <h2>Interactive Tasks with Unexpected Contacts</h2>
  <p>
    A robot performing a contact task may also be pushed by a person or encounter an obstacle elsewhere on its arm. Multi-Contact Feedback MPC
    accounts for these contacts explicitly, rather than assuming that interaction occurs only at a known end-effector location. It combines motion and
    force objectives with limits on unwanted contact forces.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/img/research/multi-contact-mpc/fig1_photo.webp' | relative_url }}"
      alt="DLR-KUKA robot performing end-effector force control while a person contacts another link"
      loading="lazy"
    >
    <figcaption>End-effector motion and force control continue while the robot responds to an additional human contact.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>An Explicit Contact Feedback Loop</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="{{ '/research/projects/mcpep/' | relative_url }}">MCP-EP</a> estimates contact locations and forces from joint torque and base
    force/torque measurements. The controller incorporates this information into a spring contact model and predicts its effect on the full
    seven-degree-of-freedom robot dynamics. Other estimators can also supply the feedback if they provide multi-contact information in real time.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/img/research/multi-contact-mpc/control_structure.webp' | relative_url }}"
      alt="Control architecture connecting MCP-EP, contact-feedback MPC, and the torque-controlled robot"
      loading="lazy"
    >
    <figcaption>Contact estimation closes the feedback loop around the predictive controller.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <h3>Contact Modeling and Optimization</h3>
  <p>
    The spring model predicts force along the estimated contact direction while leaving tangential motion unconstrained. The optimization combines
    end-effector pose tracking, contact-force regulation, and a force barrier that penalizes approaching or exceeding the permitted force.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/img/research/multi-contact-mpc/spring_contact_model.webp' | relative_url }}"
      alt="Estimated contact frame, spring stiffness, and predicted contact force"
      loading="lazy"
    >
    <figcaption>The spring contact model connects estimated contact locations to predicted interaction forces.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Box-FDDP solves the optimal control problem with analytic derivatives and a 125 ms prediction horizon. Reported update rates are 6.8 kHz without
    contact, 1.9 kHz with one contact, and 1.8 kHz with two contacts. The implementation retains the full robot dynamics without the simplifying
    assumptions discussed in the source paper.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Experiments on the DLR-KUKA LWR IV+</h2>
  <h3>Limiting an Unexpected Contact</h3>
  <p>
    When an unknown obstacle prevents the arm from reaching its target, MPC without contact feedback produces a peak force of 65.87 N. With contact
    feedback and a 15 N force limit, the peak is 14.74 N.
  </p>
  {% include research-project-video.liquid path="multi-contact-mpc/mpc_scenario1" caption="Comparison with and without contact feedback near an unknown obstacle." %}
  <h3>Hybrid Motion–Force Control</h3>
  <p>
    The robot tracks a circular end-effector trajectory while regulating contact force to 20 N. An additional human contact on the third link is
    limited to 15 N. The table reports tracking performance during the three phases of this experiment.
  </p>
